  "background_and_question": {
    "claims": [
      {
        "attribution": "Petitioner",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "23b8a204b8836873f7e4a1f94a1661b5b4eca0831e9c1819020e8c1cf7943f55",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-23b8a204b883-0001-0001"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 22,
              "start": 22
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to Petitioner, petitioners argue that Congress must have either express or implicit authority to require the State Department to list \u201cJerusalem, Israel\u201d on passports, citing prior passport cases such as Zemel v. Rusk and Kent v. Dulles."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Respondent",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "23b8a204b8836873f7e4a1f94a1661b5b4eca0831e9c1819020e8c1cf7943f55",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-23b8a204b883-0001-0004"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 24,
              "start": 24
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to Respondent, respondents contend that the Constitution gives the President exclusive power to recognize foreign sovereigns, and that this exclusive recognition power precludes Congress or the courts from dictating passport language."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Petitioner and Respondent",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "23b8a204b8836873f7e4a1f94a1661b5b4eca0831e9c1819020e8c1cf7943f55",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-23b8a204b883-0001-0001",
              "ev-23b8a204b883-0001-0004"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 24,
              "start": 22
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "The central legal question is whether Congress has the authority to require the designation \u201cJerusalem, Israel\u201d on passports, or whether that authority is exclusively vested in the President\u2019s recognition power."
      }
    ],
