              "document_hash": "23b8a204b8836873f7e4a1f94a1661b5b4eca0831e9c1819020e8c1cf7943f55",
              "evidence_ids": [
                "ev-23b8a204b883-0001-0004"
              ],
              "pages": {
                "end": 24,
                "start": 24
              }
            }
          ],
          "text": "According to Respondent, respondents assert that the Constitution grants the President exclusive recognition power, which includes deciding how a place of birth is listed on passports, and that neither Congress nor the courts may override that judgment."
        },
        {
          "attribution": "General Verrilli",
          "citations": [
            {
              "document_hash": "23b8a204b8836873f7e4a1f94a1661b5b4eca0831e9c1819020e8c1cf7943f55",
              "evidence_ids": [
                "ev-23b8a204b883-0002-0001"
              ],
              "pages": {
                "end": 40,
                "start": 40
              }
            }
          ],
          "text": "According to General Verrilli, the government further argues that the recognition power is exclusive because of timing, expertise, and the need for secrecy."
        }
      ],
